WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.934 --> 00:00:01.668
-Our first guest tonight is

00:00:01.668 --> 00:00:02.736
a United States senator

00:00:02.736 --> 00:00:04.003
from the great state
of Connecticut.

00:00:04.003 --> 00:00:06.606
Please welcome to the show
Senator Chris Murphy.

00:00:06.606 --> 00:00:08.775
[ Cheering and applause ]
Thank you for being
with us, Senator.

00:00:08.775 --> 00:00:11.511
-Thanks for having me.
Appreciate it.

00:00:11.511 --> 00:00:14.747
-Obviously not the easiest day,
but we really do appreciate

00:00:14.747 --> 00:00:17.817
you making the time to try
to put the events of yesterday

00:00:17.817 --> 00:00:20.153
in context,
if that's at all possible.

00:00:20.153 --> 00:00:22.621
You know, one of the messages
we're trying to get across today

00:00:22.621 --> 00:00:25.357
is it's not hopeless.

00:00:25.357 --> 00:00:28.161
Is that one of the things
you find yourself,

00:00:28.161 --> 00:00:30.930
as one of the leading advocates
of gun control,

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:33.032
do you find yourself running
up against people

00:00:33.032 --> 00:00:36.068
who feel like it is,
in fact, hopeless?

00:00:36.068 --> 00:00:38.570
-Of course.
In Europe at this moment,

00:00:38.570 --> 00:00:39.839
where there are
so many Americans

00:00:39.839 --> 00:00:43.776
who, you know, have become
so anesthetized, so numb,

00:00:43.776 --> 00:00:46.679
to the pace
of these mass shootings.

00:00:46.679 --> 00:00:48.882
I just can't let that happen.

00:00:48.882 --> 00:00:51.251
I can't let people
in this country believe

00:00:51.251 --> 00:00:53.620
that we can't change this,
because we can.

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:56.122
We're the only country
in the high-income world

00:00:56.122 --> 00:00:58.291
where kids fear for their lives
when they go to school.

00:00:58.291 --> 00:01:01.193
We have power over this.
We have agency.

00:01:01.193 --> 00:01:05.097
And, as I'm speaking to you,
I'm literally in touch with,

00:01:05.097 --> 00:01:07.667
you know, a half dozen or more
Republican senators

00:01:07.667 --> 00:01:09.402
that are interested
in sitting down

00:01:09.402 --> 00:01:11.170
and talking
about a path forward.

00:01:11.170 --> 00:01:12.872
I don't know whether
we'll get there,

00:01:12.872 --> 00:01:16.008
to a bipartisan product,
but I'm going to try

00:01:16.008 --> 00:01:19.846
because, as a parent
of two school-age kids,

00:01:19.846 --> 00:01:24.683
I just can't believe that we're
going to allow this to continue.

00:01:24.683 --> 00:01:26.085
And I have to believe
that we can find

00:01:26.085 --> 00:01:28.988
a handful of Republicans
in the United States Senate

00:01:28.988 --> 00:01:30.156
that are willing to work on us

00:01:30.156 --> 00:01:31.891
to make our schools
and our shopping malls

00:01:31.891 --> 00:01:35.361
and our movie theaters
just a little bit safer.

00:01:35.361 --> 00:01:38.831
-So, you were elected in 2012
and then Sandy Hook,

00:01:38.831 --> 00:01:40.666
which was obviously
in your state, happened.

00:01:40.666 --> 00:01:43.669
I feel like that was a moment
where people thought,

00:01:43.669 --> 00:01:47.140
"Surely,
something will change now."

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:50.876
How do you reflect back on the
last decade since that happened?

00:01:50.876 --> 00:01:52.111
-Well, that's what I
thought, too.

00:01:52.111 --> 00:01:55.181
I mean, I thought,
if the murder of 20 kids

00:01:55.181 --> 00:01:57.216
in an elementary school
in Connecticut

00:01:57.216 --> 00:02:00.219
doesn't change the dynamic
on this issue, what will?

00:02:00.219 --> 00:02:03.222
But I quickly changed my mind
because what I realized is

00:02:03.222 --> 00:02:05.491
that this is all
about political power.

00:02:05.491 --> 00:02:07.327
I wish it weren't, but it is.

00:02:07.327 --> 00:02:09.996
And, in 2013,
when Sandy Hook happened,

00:02:09.996 --> 00:02:12.198
the NRA had tons
of political power.

00:02:12.198 --> 00:02:14.467
The gun lobby had
tons of political power,

00:02:14.467 --> 00:02:15.935
and we had none.

00:02:15.935 --> 00:02:19.071
There really wasn't an anti-gun
violence movement in 2013.

00:02:19.071 --> 00:02:22.308
So we've spent the last ten
years building up our power.

00:02:22.308 --> 00:02:23.877
We now have
all these amazing groups --

00:02:23.877 --> 00:02:26.145
Gabby Giffords,
March for Our Lives,

00:02:26.145 --> 00:02:27.780
and Moms Demand Action --

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:29.982
and the gun lobby is weaker,
you know,

00:02:29.982 --> 00:02:31.350
they're practically bankrupt.

00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:35.755
And so we're at this moment
where maybe, maybe, maybe,

00:02:35.755 --> 00:02:37.923
the balance of political power
has shifted.

00:02:37.923 --> 00:02:40.793
Maybe enough voters care
about this

00:02:40.793 --> 00:02:42.361
that they're going
to send a message --

00:02:42.361 --> 00:02:44.197
"If you don't vote with us,
if you don't vote with our kids,

00:02:44.197 --> 00:02:45.364
we're voting you out of office."

00:02:45.364 --> 00:02:48.368
But it's taken ten years
to really equalize

00:02:48.368 --> 00:02:50.069
the political power
on the issue of guns.

00:02:50.069 --> 00:02:52.304
Back then,
we just had none of it

00:02:52.304 --> 00:02:55.675
and they had all of it
and that's why we lost.

00:02:55.675 --> 00:02:59.145
-So, you were actually presiding
on the Senate floor

00:02:59.145 --> 00:03:01.113
when this tragedy
happened yesterday

00:03:01.113 --> 00:03:03.549
and you gave your fiery speech

00:03:03.549 --> 00:03:06.018
that we've already referenced
tonight in the show.

00:03:06.018 --> 00:03:09.888
What was the energy like
in the room

00:03:09.888 --> 00:03:11.089
when you're giving that speech?

00:03:11.089 --> 00:03:12.624
Are other senators there?

00:03:12.624 --> 00:03:15.361
Do you feel like
they're listening?

00:03:15.361 --> 00:03:18.197
-I mean, a lot of people
were listening.

00:03:18.197 --> 00:03:20.566
There were only a handful
of senators there,

00:03:20.566 --> 00:03:22.568
but I know, because I've talked
to many of them,

00:03:22.568 --> 00:03:25.070
that a lot of them heard
what I said.

00:03:25.070 --> 00:03:27.207
You know, I was sitting
in the Senate chamber.

00:03:27.207 --> 00:03:28.975
I was actually presiding
over the Senate

00:03:28.975 --> 00:03:31.477
when my phone started
to blow up

00:03:31.477 --> 00:03:32.878
and I looked down to realize

00:03:32.878 --> 00:03:34.780
that we had another
Sandy Hook on our hands.

00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:38.217
And I decided to go down to the
floor immediately after

00:03:38.217 --> 00:03:41.253
and, you know, just say simply
to my colleagues, right,

00:03:41.253 --> 00:03:43.489
"Why on Earth did you spend
all this time running

00:03:43.489 --> 00:03:45.558
for the United States Senate,
if you're going to do nothing?"

00:03:45.558 --> 00:03:47.760
And I don't know
whether my colleagues

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:49.195
can be shamed
into doing something,

00:03:49.195 --> 00:03:50.663
but I'm just not going
to let them off the hook.

00:03:50.663 --> 00:03:53.566
I'm not going to let
this place do nothing

00:03:53.566 --> 00:03:58.471
and get away without at least
some rebuke on the Senate floor.

00:03:58.471 --> 00:04:00.906
So we'll see.
There's a lot of Republicans,

00:04:00.906 --> 00:04:03.175
because of what I said
and because

00:04:03.175 --> 00:04:05.277
of what their constituents
have said to them in phone calls

00:04:05.277 --> 00:04:06.846
and emails
over the last 24 hours,

00:04:06.846 --> 00:04:08.147
that are reaching out
to work with us

00:04:08.147 --> 00:04:10.516
and we'll see
if we can get something done.

00:04:10.516 --> 00:04:12.618
-It seems like Republicans
do a very good job

00:04:12.618 --> 00:04:16.990
of framing this issue
in a fear-based way of,

00:04:16.990 --> 00:04:18.357
"They're coming
to take away your guns."

00:04:18.357 --> 00:04:21.627
There are many versions
of legislation

00:04:21.627 --> 00:04:23.262
where that will not be the case.

00:04:23.262 --> 00:04:28.034
There will be modifications,
there will be steps,

00:04:28.034 --> 00:04:30.269
you know, closing loopholes,

00:04:30.269 --> 00:04:32.772
things like that,
banning assault rifles.

00:04:32.772 --> 00:04:35.941
Why do you feel like they're so
strong at framing the narrative?

00:04:35.941 --> 00:04:40.112
And it strikes me that Democrats
have done a poorer job.

00:04:40.112 --> 00:04:41.513
-Well, you know,
that's interesting,

00:04:41.513 --> 00:04:43.049
that you put it that way,
because, actually,

00:04:43.049 --> 00:04:45.351
if you look
at the polling on this,

00:04:45.351 --> 00:04:47.352
there are very few issues
in America

00:04:47.352 --> 00:04:51.323
in which 90% of people in this
country agree on anything

00:04:51.323 --> 00:04:53.259
and 90% of Americans think
that you should have

00:04:53.259 --> 00:04:54.360
universal background checks.

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:55.795
Actually, the majority
of Republicans

00:04:55.795 --> 00:04:59.565
now support
an assault weapons ban.

00:04:59.565 --> 00:05:03.236
The problem is very much just,
in this town,

00:05:03.236 --> 00:05:04.937
Republicans,
for whatever reason,

00:05:04.937 --> 00:05:08.775
are so afraid of the gun lobby,
the NRA,

00:05:08.775 --> 00:05:11.010
that they can't get
out of their own way.

00:05:11.010 --> 00:05:12.845
So I actually think we've
won this debate

00:05:12.845 --> 00:05:14.013
in the American public.

00:05:14.013 --> 00:05:16.048
We just need voters, now,
to do more

00:05:16.048 --> 00:05:18.150
than sort of click
the right answer on a poll.

00:05:18.150 --> 00:05:21.220
We need voters to say, "Hey,
in this next election,

00:05:21.220 --> 00:05:22.689
I'm going to ask
the candidates,

00:05:22.689 --> 00:05:24.656
'Do you support banning
assault weapons?

00:05:24.656 --> 00:05:26.359
Do you support universal
background checks?'

00:05:26.359 --> 00:05:30.329
and not vote for the folks
that give the wrong answer."

00:05:30.329 --> 00:05:33.166
-You talk about the fact
that you've won on the issue,

00:05:33.166 --> 00:05:36.468
but you've also been critical
of Democrats

00:05:36.468 --> 00:05:38.070
not wanting to run on it.

00:05:38.070 --> 00:05:41.273
Don't you feel like there is --

00:05:41.273 --> 00:05:43.342
If the GOP's afraid
of the gun lobby,

00:05:43.342 --> 00:05:46.712
it strikes me that the Democrats
are afraid of something

00:05:46.712 --> 00:05:49.581
because they don't run on it
in a way that --

00:05:49.581 --> 00:05:52.217
certainly not as strongly
as you lay it out.

00:05:52.217 --> 00:05:56.755
-Yeah, we're sort of stuck
in a world that doesn't exist.

00:05:56.755 --> 00:05:59.725
Democrats think
that we will lose votes

00:05:59.725 --> 00:06:04.129
if we talk about gun violence,
and that's just not true.

00:06:04.129 --> 00:06:06.032
In fact, in 2018,

00:06:06.032 --> 00:06:08.835
record numbers of young people
turned out in that election.

00:06:08.835 --> 00:06:11.704
Democrats won control of the
House of Representatives. Why?

00:06:11.704 --> 00:06:14.406
Because we talked about the
issue of gun violence.

00:06:14.406 --> 00:06:17.910
So, the NRA has kind of created
this mythology

00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:20.446
that running on things
like background checks

00:06:20.446 --> 00:06:21.580
is a losing political issue.

00:06:21.580 --> 00:06:23.382
It's the exact opposite.

00:06:23.382 --> 00:06:24.951
It actually convinces
a lot of people

00:06:24.951 --> 00:06:27.186
who weren't planning
on turning out to turn out,

00:06:27.186 --> 00:06:30.756
and it's a lot of swing voters
who want to hear Democrats,

00:06:30.756 --> 00:06:32.958
want to hear candidates,
talk about

00:06:32.958 --> 00:06:35.428
the most pressing moral issue
that they and their kids face,

00:06:35.428 --> 00:06:38.130
which, right now, is the fear
that our kids have

00:06:38.130 --> 00:06:40.065
for their lives
when they walk into schools.

00:06:40.065 --> 00:06:41.200
We have to talk about this

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:43.469
and we'll win votes
if we talk about it.

00:06:43.469 --> 00:06:46.472
-Sometimes I think there's
a frustration at a distance --

00:06:46.472 --> 00:06:49.709
maybe people don't understand
quite how it works in D.C. --

00:06:49.709 --> 00:06:51.977
at the speed
in which things happen.

00:06:51.977 --> 00:06:57.049
It always strikes me as a little
bit slower than it should,

00:06:57.049 --> 00:06:58.884
especially after a tragedy
like this.

00:06:58.884 --> 00:07:01.654
When do you see,
when you predict,

00:07:01.654 --> 00:07:04.123
any vote coming to the floor
that would actually

00:07:04.123 --> 00:07:07.526
force Republicans
to choose a side to land on?

00:07:07.526 --> 00:07:09.896
-Well, the other thing
to sort of note here is

00:07:09.896 --> 00:07:12.598
that the reason
why it's taking so long is

00:07:12.598 --> 00:07:14.700
because of the rules
in the Senate, right?

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:18.337
You've got this rule that says
50 votes isn't good enough.

00:07:18.337 --> 00:07:19.405
You need 60 votes.

00:07:19.405 --> 00:07:21.240
So the voters kind of
did their job.

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:23.075
They elected a majority
in the Senate,

00:07:23.075 --> 00:07:26.178
a majority in the House,
and a president who all support

00:07:26.178 --> 00:07:28.047
something like
universal background checks.

00:07:28.047 --> 00:07:32.251
The problem is we have 50 votes
in the Senate, not 60 votes.

00:07:32.251 --> 00:07:34.487
So, you know, part of what
has to happen here is

00:07:34.487 --> 00:07:36.389
that we change the rules
of the Senate so that,

00:07:36.389 --> 00:07:39.391
if the voters put majorities
in charge of everything,

00:07:39.391 --> 00:07:41.961
then they actually get
what they are asking for.

00:07:41.961 --> 00:07:43.228
And, in this case,
they're asking

00:07:43.228 --> 00:07:44.363
for universal background checks

00:07:44.363 --> 00:07:45.465
and they're only not getting it

00:07:45.465 --> 00:07:48.301
because we've got this silly
sort of

00:07:48.301 --> 00:07:51.737
19-, you know, -50s-era rule
in the Senate

00:07:51.737 --> 00:07:53.872
that requires 60 votes.

00:07:53.872 --> 00:07:57.609
-Obviously, from your time

00:07:57.609 --> 00:08:01.813
visiting, speaking with people
in the community of Sandy Hook,

00:08:01.813 --> 00:08:06.686
you are acutely aware that maybe
the rest of us move on,

00:08:06.686 --> 00:08:12.024
but communities where something
like this happen never do.

00:08:12.024 --> 00:08:13.292
-Never do.

00:08:13.358 --> 00:08:14.427
Never do.

00:08:14.427 --> 00:08:16.895
Sandy Hook will never,
ever be the same.

00:08:16.895 --> 00:08:19.398
Because the research tells us,

00:08:19.398 --> 00:08:21.934
for every individual
that's killed,

00:08:21.934 --> 00:08:23.836
there are 20 people
who know them,

00:08:23.836 --> 00:08:27.172
20 relatives and friends
who experience trauma.

00:08:27.172 --> 00:08:29.074
You know,
I tell the story in Newtown.

00:08:29.074 --> 00:08:31.577
I told it on the floor
just last night.

00:08:31.577 --> 00:08:34.180
You know, afterwards, when those
kids came back to that school,

00:08:34.180 --> 00:08:36.749
they had to have
this procedure in place that,

00:08:36.749 --> 00:08:40.219
when a kid had a memory
of what he saw that day,

00:08:40.219 --> 00:08:42.688
you know,
stepping over his dead bodies --

00:08:42.688 --> 00:08:44.823
his classmates' dead bodies,

00:08:44.823 --> 00:08:46.792
they would scream out
a safe word

00:08:46.792 --> 00:08:49.128
and that paraprofessionals
and psychologists

00:08:49.128 --> 00:08:50.663
and psychiatrists would come
and help them.

00:08:50.663 --> 00:08:52.631
In one classroom,
that word was monkey.

00:08:52.631 --> 00:08:54.400
And so all throughout the day,

00:08:54.400 --> 00:08:56.368
kids would be screaming out,
"Monkey! Monkey!"

00:08:56.368 --> 00:08:59.504
And paraprofessionals
would come and help them.

00:08:59.504 --> 00:09:02.508
That's just a sign
of how devastating this is,

00:09:02.508 --> 00:09:05.578
how the pain never goes away
in a place like Newtown.

00:09:05.578 --> 00:09:08.647
It's never going to go away
in Uvalde.

00:09:08.647 --> 00:09:11.083
And we just have
a moral obligation,

00:09:11.083 --> 00:09:12.417
as a nation,
to try to make sure

00:09:12.417 --> 00:09:14.753
that this doesn't happen
to another community

00:09:14.753 --> 00:09:16.989
because you don't recover
from this.

00:09:16.989 --> 00:09:18.457
As a family, you don't recover

00:09:18.457 --> 00:09:19.759
and, as a community,
you don't recover.

00:09:19.759 --> 00:09:21.560
So why on Earth do we allow

00:09:21.560 --> 00:09:24.997
for communities
to still go through this?

00:09:24.997 --> 00:09:28.000
-I'd love -- I would really love
and appreciate

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:31.270
if you could leave us
with any path forward,

00:09:31.270 --> 00:09:32.571
any reason for optimism,

00:09:32.571 --> 00:09:34.573
anything those of us watching

00:09:34.573 --> 00:09:39.544
who believe that there is some
step of gun control we can take,

00:09:39.544 --> 00:09:40.913
what we can be doing.

00:09:40.913 --> 00:09:42.181
-Well, I just want to make sure
people know

00:09:42.181 --> 00:09:44.483
lots of things have changed
since Sandy Hook.

00:09:44.483 --> 00:09:48.187
In fact, there have been dozens
of states that have passed laws

00:09:48.187 --> 00:09:51.824
that have tightened gun laws
and have saved lives.

00:09:51.824 --> 00:09:55.193
The NRA is weaker
than they were in 2013.

00:09:55.193 --> 00:09:57.429
The gun violence movement
is stronger.

00:09:57.429 --> 00:10:00.599
When Republicans had control
of the entire Congress,

00:10:00.599 --> 00:10:03.369
the NRA got nothing
from the Republican Congress.

00:10:03.369 --> 00:10:04.370
They used to get everything

00:10:04.370 --> 00:10:05.871
when Republicans were in charge.
Why?

00:10:05.871 --> 00:10:08.707
Because Republicans maybe aren't
willing, yet, to vote with us,

00:10:08.707 --> 00:10:11.110
but they're not willing
any longer to vote with the NRA.

00:10:11.110 --> 00:10:13.945
I know that people want this all
to happen overnight,

00:10:13.945 --> 00:10:16.982
but it took ten years
from when James Brady was shot

00:10:16.982 --> 00:10:19.585
during the assassination attempt
to President Reagan

00:10:19.585 --> 00:10:23.588
until the James Brady background
check law was passed.

00:10:23.588 --> 00:10:24.823
It took over ten years

00:10:24.823 --> 00:10:26.859
from the open casket
of Emmett Till

00:10:26.859 --> 00:10:28.861
until the Civil Rights Act
was passed.

00:10:28.861 --> 00:10:32.164
Sometimes, in this country,
it does take a decade or more

00:10:32.164 --> 00:10:33.965
to get great
social change passed.

00:10:33.965 --> 00:10:35.501
And we have made
a lot of progress,

00:10:35.501 --> 00:10:37.936
more progress than people think
and, boy,

00:10:37.936 --> 00:10:40.772
there's no more important issue
to continue to stay active on.

00:10:40.772 --> 00:10:43.175
Don't let obstacles stand
in the way of your advocacy.

00:10:43.175 --> 00:10:45.077
We will get this done,
in the end.

00:10:45.077 --> 00:10:46.278
-Senator,
I cannot thank you enough

00:10:46.278 --> 00:10:48.213
for giving us your time today.

00:10:48.213 --> 00:10:51.317
Keep up the good fight
and we really appreciate it.

00:10:51.317 --> 00:10:53.152
-Thanks, Seth.
[ Cheering and applause ]

00:10:53.152 --> 00:10:55.121
-Senator Chris Murphy,
everybody. We'll be right back.

